From e002e92b7cf46ee57f5694d1f4d9b88e0a555d00 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vuln Patch Pipeline <vuln-patch@chainguard.dev>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 14:09:07 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Cap the size of decompressed JWT payloads

DefaultJwtParser.parse(String) decompresses a token's payload as soon as
the header names a compression algorithm, before any signature check. The
shipped DeflateCompressionCodec and GzipCompressionCodec decompressed with
no size limit, so a small unauthenticated token carrying a decompression
bomb could expand to hundreds of megabytes and exhaust heap and CPU
(CWE-409, CWE-400).

Both codecs now write their output through a bounded buffer in
AbstractCompressionCodec. The running total of produced bytes is checked
on every write, before the bytes are stored, and decompression aborts with
an IOException, surfaced as CompressionException, once it would exceed the
limit. The default limit is 1 MiB. The JVM system property
io.jsonwebtoken.compression.maxDecompressedBytes, read once at class load,
overrides it: a positive value sets the limit in bytes, 0 or less disables
it, and a non-numeric value is ignored. Setting it logs a warning.

Compression, uncompressed tokens, signature verification and the parser's
order of operations are unchanged.

Adds ZipBombExploitTest and ZipBombEvasionTest.

Cgp-Finding: CGP-753c-3hg7-j33f
